  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The solution to $\\frac{16}{x}=8$ is$\\,\\,x\\,=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution. This is because if $x=0,$ then the rational term would be $\\frac{16}{0},$ which is undefined because it has a zero denominator.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $x$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16}{x} & =8 \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{16}{x} & =x\\cdot 8 \\\\ \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{16}{\\cancel{x}} & =8x \\\\ 16 & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =8x \\\\ \\frac{16}{8} & =\\frac{8x}{8} \\\\ 2 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=2$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=2.$",
